    dict(key='01-poster', kind='poster', title='JWT Done Right with Nimbus JOSE + JWT',
         body=None,
         narration="Hello, and welcome. [[slnc 400]] This video explains J W T with Nimbus, in Java. [[slnc 300]] This video is presented by Jayasekhar Konduru. [[slnc 600]] First, a simple definition. [[slnc 300]] With Nimbus J O S E + J W T an A P I verifies a bearer token by fixing the algorithm and the type, taking keys only from the one key set U R L in its settings, and letting the library check the signature, expiry, issuer, audience and required claims; but the scope, the lifetime policy, the cache time of the key set and the fact that a token cannot be taken back remain the A P I's own decisions. [[slnc 600]] Think of it this way. [[slnc 300]] A bank teller who has to check a cheque. The printing, the signature and the date can be checked by anyone with the right stamp and a reference book, and a library is that stamp and that book. Whether this cheque is for this bank, today, for this much, is still the teller's decision. [[slnc 700]] In this video, the domain is an online bank. [[slnc 500]] By the end, you will be able to: [[slnc 300]] Configure a Nimbus processor with a fixed algorithm, type, key source and claims verifier. [[slnc 300]] Explain why jku and jwk headers are never followed. [[slnc 300]] Plan a key rotation around the cache time."),
